i.github.com/users/Foyezes/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Foyezes/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-03-30T12:16:03Z","updated_at":"2026-03-30T12:16:03Z","body":"> I just ran into this problem. Doesnt seem to matter what type of varyings are declared, as long as theres more than 17 the shader will fail to compile and give an error in the output that says \"Too many varyings used in shader (33 used, maximum supported is 32)\" however it will not give the error in the shader editor till you actually have more than 32 varyings declared.\n\nI didn't know it gave an error at 32 varyings.\n\nBut you can combine non vec4 varyings into vec4 ones to send more data. If your varyings are mostly floats or vec2's you can send 4x to 2x more data if you combine them into vec4's. I think this works more like slots instead of how many vectors you are sending, unlike uniforms.","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4154596273/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/4419242639","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/116037#issuecomment-4419242639","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/116037","id":4419242639